INIT(8)             UNIX Programmer's Manual             INIT(8)



NAME
     init - process control initialization

SYNOPSIS
     init

DESCRIPTION
     Init is the first userland process, pid 1.  The kernel starts
     it at boot; nothing starts it afterwards.

     Init first changes directory to /root, then runs

          sh /etc/rc

     and waits for it to finish.  The whole of system startup
     happens there -- notably /etc/netstart, where dhclient
     acquires a lease -- so no terminal is offered until it is
     done.  A missing or failing /etc/rc is not fatal; init
     carries on.

     Init then reads /etc/ttys (see ttys(5)) and builds a table of
     every line marked "on".  For each it runs that line's getty
     command and remembers the pid.  Thereafter init waits for
     children to exit.  When a reaped pid matches a line, that
     line's command is started again; a pid that matches no line
     is an orphan reparented to init and is simply reaped.

     The console line is started with init's own file descriptors,
     which already are the console.  Every other line is opened as
     /dev/<name> onto the child's descriptors 0, 1 and 2 before
     the command runs, so getty(8) opens nothing itself.

     A line whose command is a bare shell -- which is what the
     console line ships as -- is given PATH, HOME and TERM, since
     no login(1) runs in between to build them.  A line that runs
     getty(8) gets its environment from login(1) instead.

     Init exits when no line is left to keep alive.  Since init
     has no parent, the kernel halts the machine.

RESPAWN THROTTLE
     A command that exits immediately would otherwise be started
     forever.  If a line is started more than five times inside
     sixty seconds, init disables it and reports

          init: <line>: '<cmd>' respawning too fast -- line disabled

     A disabled line is never started again.  If that leaves no
     line running, init exits and the machine halts, rather than
     spinning invisibly.

SIGNALS
     SIGTERM shuts the system down in an orderly way.  Init reports

          init: shutting down

     stops respawning, hangs up every line still running, calls
     sync(2), and exits -- and since init has no parent, that
     exit is what halts the machine.  Nothing else needs to sync(2)
     before signalling init.

     SIGHUP re-reads /etc/ttys and merges it into the running set
     rather than rebuilding it:

     o   a line that is unchanged is left strictly alone, session
         and all;

     o   a line that is new, or whose command changed, is started
         (the old command's child is hung up first);

     o   a line that has left the file, or that flipped to "off",
         has its child hung up and is retired, with

              init: <line>: no longer enabled

     A SIGHUP also clears the respawn throttle on every line still
     listed "on", so a line disabled by the throttle can be brought
     back by fixing its command and signalling init -- no reboot.

     Lines are hung up with SIGHUP, not SIGTERM.  An interactive
     Bourne shell ignores SIGTERM deliberately (sh(1) treats signal
     15 as uncatchable-by-accident for a login shell), so SIGTERM
     against a shell line does nothing at all; SIGHUP is both the
     signal it honours and the correct meaning, since the line is
     going away.

     A signal arriving while /etc/rc is still running is not caught;
     SIGTERM there keeps its default action of terminating init.

FILES
     /etc/rc               system startup script
     /etc/ttys             lines and the programs to run on them

SEE ALSO
     getty(8), login(1), sh(1), ttys(5), signal(2)

BUGS
     There is no single-user mode.



